= There's Plenty of Room at the Bottom / 底部还有很大空间

#html.p(id: "en-S001")[#text("I imagine experimental physicists must often look with envy at men like Kamerlingh Onnes, who discovered a field like low temperature, which seems to be bottomless and in which one can go down and down. Such a man is then a leader and has some temporary monopoly in a scientific adventure. Percy Bridgman, in designing a way to obtain higher pressures, opened up another new field and was able to move into it and to lead us all along. The development of ever higher vacuum was a continuing development of the same kind.")]

#zh(id: "S001")[#text("我想，实验物理学家一定常常羡慕卡末林·昂内斯这样的人。他发现了低温这样一个领域，似乎深不见底，可以不断向下探索。这样的人便成为领路者，在一场科学探险中暂时拥有某种独占地位。珀西·布里奇曼设计出获得更高压力的方法，开辟了另一个新领域，并得以深入其中，带领我们所有人前进。不断提高真空度，也是同类的持续发展。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S002")[#text("I would like to describe a field, in which little has been done, but in which an enormous amount can be done in principle. This field is not quite the same as the others in that it will not tell us much of fundamental physics (in the sense of, “What are the strange particles?”) but it is more like solid-state physics in the sense that it might tell us much of great interest about the strange phenomena that occur in complex situations. Furthermore, a point that is most important is that it would have an enormous number of technical applications.")]

#zh(id: "S002")[#text("我想描述一个领域：我们在其中做得很少，但原则上能做的事极其多。它与其他领域不完全一样，因为它不会告诉我们太多基础物理知识——例如‘奇异粒子是什么？’——而更像固体物理：它可能告诉我们许多复杂情形下出现的奇特现象，非常有趣。此外，最重要的一点是，它将拥有极其大量的技术应用。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S003")[#text("What I want to talk about is the problem of manipulating and controlling things on a small scale.")]

#zh(id: "S003")[#text("我要谈的，是在微小尺度上操纵和控制事物的问题。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S004")[#text("As soon as I mention this, people tell me about miniaturization, and how far it has progressed today. They tell me about electric motors that are the size of the nail on your small finger. And there is a device on the market, they tell me, by which you can write the Lord’s Prayer on the head of a pin. But that’s nothing; that’s the most primitive, halting step in the direction I intend to discuss. It is a staggeringly small world that is below. In the year 2000, when they look back at this age, they will wonder why it was not until the year 1960 that anybody began seriously to move in this direction.")]

#zh(id: "S004")[#text("我一提到这一点，人们便告诉我微型化的情况，以及它今天已经取得多大进展。他们说，有电动机小得只有小指指甲那么大；市场上还有一种装置，可以把《主祷文》写在针头上。但那算不了什么；在我打算讨论的方向上，那只是最原始、最蹒跚的一步。下面是一个小得令人震惊的世界。到了2000年，人们回望这个时代，会纳闷：为什么直到1960年，才有人开始认真向这个方向迈进？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S005")[#text("Why cannot we write the entire 24 volumes of the Encyclopedia Brittanica on the head of a pin?")]

#zh(id: "S005")[#text("为什么不能把整套24卷《大英百科全书》写在一个针头上呢？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S006")[#text("Let’s see what would be involved. The head of a pin is a sixteenth of an inch across. If you magnify it by 25,000 diameters, the area of the head of the pin is then equal to the area of all the pages of the Encyclopaedia Brittanica. Therefore, all it is necessary to do is to reduce in size all the writing in the Encyclopaedia by 25,000 times. Is that possible? The resolving power of the eye is about 1/120 of an inch — that is roughly the diameter of one of the little dots on the fine half-tone reproductions in the Encyclopaedia. This, when you demagnify it by 25,000 times, is still 80 angstroms in diameter — 32 atoms across, in an ordinary metal. In other words, one of those dots still would contain in its area 1,000 atoms. So, each dot can easily be adjusted in size as required by the photoengraving, and there is no question that there is enough room on the head of a pin to put all of the Encyclopaedia Brittanica.")]

#zh(id: "S006")[#text("看看这需要些什么。一枚针头的直径是十六分之一英寸。如果把它的直径放大25000倍，针头的面积便等于《大英百科全书》全部页面的面积。所以，只需把百科全书里的全部文字缩小25000倍。可能吗？眼睛的分辨能力大约是1/120英寸，大致等于百科全书精细网目图片中一个小网点的直径。把它缩小25000倍后，直径仍有80埃；在普通金属中，相当于横向排列32个原子。换句话说，一个这样的网点，其面积内仍有1000个原子。因此，每个网点的大小都很容易按照相制版的要求调整。毫无疑问，一个针头上有足够的空间容纳整套《大英百科全书》。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S007")[#text("Furthermore, it can be read if it is so written. Let’s imagine that it is written in raised letters of metal; that is, where the black is in the Encyclopedia, we have raised letters of metal that are actually 1/25,000 of their ordinary size. How would we read it?")]

#zh(id: "S007")[#text("而且，如果这样写出来，它就可以读出来。设想我们用凸起的金属字来写：百科全书中原来黑色的地方，对应着凸起的金属字，其大小只有通常字的1/25000。我们怎样读呢？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S008")[#text("If we had something written in such a way, we could read it using techniques in common use today. (They will undoubtedly find a better way when we do actually have it written, but to make my point conservatively I shall just take techniques we know today.) We would press the metal into a plastic material and make a mold of it, then peel the plastic off very carefully, evaporate silica into the plastic to get a very thin film, then shadow it by evaporating gold at an angle against the silica so that all the little letters will appear clearly, dissolve the plastic away from the silica film, and then look through it with an electron microscope!")]

#zh(id: "S008")[#text("如果已经这样写好了，用今天常用的技术就能阅读。（等到真的写出来时，肯定会找到更好的办法；但为了稳妥说明我的观点，我只采用今天已经知道的技术。）把金属压进塑料材料里，制成模具，然后极其小心地剥下塑料，向塑料上蒸镀二氧化硅，得到一层很薄的膜；再从斜方向向二氧化硅蒸镀金，做成投影，使所有小字都能清晰显现；接着从二氧化硅薄膜上溶去塑料，最后用电子显微镜观察这层膜！")]

#html.p(id: "en-S009")[#text("There is no question that if the thing were reduced by 25,000 times in the form of raised letters on the pin, it would be easy for us to read it today. Furthermore; there is no question that we would find it easy to make copies of the master; we would just need to press the same metal plate again into plastic and we would have another copy.")]

#zh(id: "S009")[#text("毫无疑问，如果内容缩小25000倍，以凸字的形式写在针头上，今天我们就很容易读出来。而且，也毫无疑问，我们很容易复制这个母版：只需再把同一块金属版压进塑料，就会得到另一份副本。")]

=== How do we write small? / 怎样写得这么小？

#html.p(id: "en-S011")[#text("The next question is: How do we write it? We have no standard technique to do this now. But let me argue that it is not as difficult as it first appears to be. We can reverse the lenses of the electron microscope in order to demagnify as well as magnify. A source of ions, sent through the microscope lenses in reverse, could be focused to a very small spot. We could write with that spot like we write in a TV cathode ray oscilloscope, by going across in lines, and having an adjustment which determines the amount of material which is going to be deposited as we scan in lines.")]

#zh(id: "S011")[#text("下一个问题是：怎样把它写出来？目前还没有标准技术。但我要说明，这没有初看起来那么困难。把电子显微镜的透镜系统反过来使用，就既可以放大，也可以缩小。让一个离子源反向通过显微镜透镜，便可以聚焦成极小的点。我们可以像电视阴极射线示波器写图像那样，用这个点逐行扫描；同时调整扫描过程中沉积的材料量。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S012")[#text("This method might be very slow because of space charge limitations. There will be more rapid methods. We could first make, perhaps by some photo process, a screen which has holes in it in the form of the letters. Then we would strike an arc behind the holes and draw metallic ions through the holes; then we could again use our system of lenses and make a small image in the form of ions, which would deposit the metal on the pin.")]

#zh(id: "S012")[#text("这种方法可能因空间电荷的限制而非常慢。还会有更快的方法。我们可以先做一块屏板，也许用某种照相工艺，让孔洞组成文字的形状。然后在孔后点起电弧，把金属离子引过孔洞；再利用透镜系统，把离子形成的字形影像缩小，让金属沉积在针头上。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S013")[#text("A simpler way might be this (though I am not sure it would work): We take light and, through an optical microscope running backwards, we focus it onto a very small photoelectric screen. Then electrons come away from the screen where the light is shining. These electrons are focused down in size by the electron microscope lenses to impinge directly upon the surface of the metal. Will such a beam etch away the metal if it is run long enough? I don’t know. If it doesn’t work for a metal surface, it must be possible to find some surface with which to coat the original pin so that, where the electrons bombard, a change is made which we could recognize later.")]

#zh(id: "S013")[#text("一种更简单的办法可能如下，虽然我不确定它是否有效：让光反向通过光学显微镜，聚焦到一块极小的光电屏上。光照到哪里，哪里就发射电子。这些电子经电子显微镜透镜聚焦、缩小，直接打到金属表面。这样一束电子，如果持续足够久，能蚀去金属吗？我不知道。如果对金属表面不起作用，应该能找到某种材料，涂在原来的针头上，使被电子轰击的地方发生一种以后可以辨认的变化。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S014")[#text("There is no intensity problem in these devices — not what you are used to in magnification, where you have to take a few electrons and spread them over a bigger and bigger screen; it is just the opposite. The light which we get from a page is concentrated onto a very small area so it is very intense. The few electrons which come from the photoelectric screen are demagnified down to a very tiny area so that, again, they are very intense. I don’t know why this hasn’t been done yet!")]

#zh(id: "S014")[#text("这些装置不存在强度问题，不像放大时通常遇到的情况：把少量电子摊到越来越大的屏幕上。这里恰好相反。来自一个页面的光，被集中到极小的面积上，因此强度很高。光电屏发出的少量电子又被缩聚到极微小的面积上，所以同样具有很高的强度。我不明白，为什么这还没有人做！")]

#html.p(id: "en-S015")[#text("That’s the Encyclopaedia Brittanica on the head of a pin, but let’s consider all the books in the world. The Library of Congress has approximately 9 million volumes; the British Museum Library has 5 million volumes; there are also 5 million volumes in the National Library in France. Undoubtedly there are duplications, so let us say that there are some 24 million volumes of interest in the world.")]

#zh(id: "S015")[#text("这就是把《大英百科全书》写在针头上的办法。但再想想全世界所有的书。美国国会图书馆约有900万卷，大英博物馆图书馆有500万卷，法国国家图书馆也有500万卷。其中当然有重复，因此我们就说，世界上值得保存的书有约2400万卷。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S016")[#text("What would happen if I print all this down at the scale we have been discussing? How much space would it take? It would take, of course, the area of about a million pinheads because, instead of there being just the 24 volumes of the Encyclopaedia, there are 24 million volumes. The million pinheads can be put in a square of a thousand pins on a side, or an area of about 3 square yards. That is to say, the silica replica with the paper-thin backing of plastic, with which we have made the copies, with all this information, is on an area of approximately the size of 35 pages of the Encyclopaedia. That is about half as many pages as there are in this magazine. All of the information which all of mankind has every recorded in books can be carried around in a pamphlet in your hand — and not written in code, but a simple reproduction of the original pictures, engravings, and everything else on a small scale without loss of resolution.")]

#zh(id: "S016")[#text("如果把这些书全部按前面讨论的比例缩印，会怎样？需要多少空间？当然，大约要一百万个针头的面积，因为现在不是百科全书的24卷，而是2400万卷。这一百万个针头，可以排成每边一千个的正方形，面积大约3平方码。也就是说，带着纸一样薄的塑料底层、用于制作副本的二氧化硅复制膜，容纳全部这些信息后，面积也只相当于百科全书的35页左右，约为这本杂志页数的一半。人类记录在所有书籍中的全部信息，都可以放在手中的一本小册子里带走；而且不是编码后的内容，而是原来图片、版画和一切其他内容的直接缩小复制，分辨率毫无损失。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S017")[#text("What would our librarian at Caltech say, as she runs all over from one building to another, if I tell her that, ten years from now, all of the information that she is struggling to keep track of — 120,000 volumes, stacked from the floor to the ceiling, drawers full of cards, storage rooms full of the older books — can be kept on just one library card! When the University of Brazil, for example, finds that their library is burned, we can send them a copy of every book in our library by striking off a copy from the master plate in a few hours and mailing it in an envelope no bigger or heavier than any other ordinary air mail letter.")]

#zh(id: "S017")[#text("如果我告诉加州理工的图书管理员：十年后，她在各座楼之间奔走、苦苦管理的全部信息——从地板堆到天花板的十二万卷书、装满卡片的抽屉、堆着旧书的储藏室——都可以存放在一张借书卡上，她会怎么说呢？譬如，巴西某所大学发现图书馆烧毁了，我们几小时就能从母版印出一份我们馆藏每一本书的副本，装进一个与普通航空信件大小、重量无异的信封寄给他们。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S018")[#text("Now, the name of this talk is “There is Plenty of Room at the Bottom” — not just “There is Room at the Bottom.” What I have demonstrated is that there is room — that you can decrease the size of things in a practical way. I now want to show that there is plenty of room. I will not now discuss how we are going to do it, but only what is possible in principle — in other words, what is possible according to the laws of physics. I am not inventing anti-gravity, which is possible someday only if the laws are not what we think. I am telling you what could be done if the laws are what we think; we are not doing it simply because we haven’t yet gotten around to it.")]

#zh(id: "S018")[#text("这次演讲的题目是‘底部还有很大空间’，不只是‘底部还有空间’。我刚证明的是有空间：可以用实际可行的方法缩小东西。现在我要证明，空间还很大。我先不讨论怎样实现，只谈原则上可能做到什么，也就是物理定律允许什么。我不是在发明反重力；那种东西只有在物理定律与我们的认识不同的情况下，将来才可能实现。我讲的是，如果定律就是我们所想的那样，我们能够做到什么。我们没有做，仅仅因为还没有着手去做。")]

=== Information on a small scale / 微小尺度上的信息

#html.p(id: "en-S020")[#text("Suppose that, instead of trying to reproduce the pictures and all the information directly in its present form, we write only the information content in a code of dots and dashes, or something like that, to represent the various letters. Each letter represents six or seven “bits” of information; that is, you need only about six or seven dots or dashes for each letter. Now, instead of writing everything, as I did before, on the surface of the head of a pin, I am going to use the interior of the material as well.")]

#zh(id: "S020")[#text("假设我们不试图直接按现有形式复制图片和全部信息，而是只写下信息内容，用点划之类的编码表示不同字母。每个字母代表六七个‘比特’的信息，也就是说，每个字母只需六七个点或划。现在，我不再像前面那样把一切写在针头表面，还要利用材料的内部。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S021")[#text("Let us represent a dot by a small spot of one metal, the next dash, by an adjacent spot of another metal, and so on. Suppose, to be conservative, that a bit of information is going to require a little cube of atoms 5 times 5 times 5 — that is 125 atoms. Perhaps we need a hundred and some odd atoms to make sure that the information is not lost through diffusion, or through some other process.")]

#zh(id: "S021")[#text("我们用某一种金属的一个小点表示‘点’，用旁边另一种金属的小点表示下一个‘划’，如此类推。保守地假设，每个比特需要一个5×5×5个原子的微小立方体，也就是125个原子。或许需要一百多个原子，才能确保信息不因扩散或其他过程而丢失。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S022")[#text("I have estimated how many letters there are in the Encyclopaedia, and I have assumed that each of my 24 million books is as big as an Encyclopaedia volume, and have calculated, then, how many bits of information there are (10^15). For each bit I allow 100 atoms. And it turns out that all of the information that man has carefully accumulated in all the books in the world can be written in this form in a cube of material one two-hundredth of an inch wide — which is the barest piece of dust that can be made out by the human eye. So there is plenty of room at the bottom! Don’t tell me about microfilm!")]

#zh(id: "S022")[#text("我估算了百科全书有多少字母，并假定2400万本书每本都和百科全书的一卷一样厚，由此算出总共有多少比特的信息：10¹⁵。每个比特我留出100个原子。结果，全世界所有书籍中人类精心积累的全部信息，都可以用这种形式，写在一个边长1/200英寸的材料立方体里；那只是人眼勉强能看清的一粒灰尘。所以，底部还有很大空间！别再跟我说缩微胶片了！")]

#html.p(id: "en-S023")[#text("This fact — that enormous amounts of information can be carried in an exceedingly small space — is, of course, well known to the biologists, and resolves the mystery which existed before we understood all this clearly, of how it could be that, in the tiniest cell, all of the information for the organization of a complex creature such as ourselves can be stored. All this information — whether we have brown eyes, or whether we think at all, or that in the embryo the jawbone should first develop with a little hole in the side so that later a nerve can grow through it — all this information is contained in a very tiny fraction of the cell in the form of long-chain DNA molecules in which approximately 50 atoms are used for one bit of information about the cell.")]

#zh(id: "S023")[#text("极其微小的空间能携带海量信息，这一点，生物学家当然早就知道。它解决了一个谜：在清楚理解这一点之前，我们不明白，最小的细胞里，怎么能够存储组织像我们这样复杂生物的全部信息。这些信息——眼睛是否为棕色，我们是否能够思考，或者胚胎的颌骨为什么要先在一侧发育出一个小孔，好让神经以后穿过去——全都以长链DNA分子的形式，包含在细胞中极小的一部分里。有关细胞的每个比特信息，大约使用50个原子。")]

=== Better electron microscopes / 更好的电子显微镜

#html.p(id: "en-S025")[#text("If I have written in a code, with 5 times 5 times 5 atoms to a bit, the question is: How could I read it today? The electron microscope is not quite good enough, with the greatest care and effort, it can only resolve about 10 angstroms. I would like to try and impress upon you while I am talking about all of these things on a small scale, the importance of improving the electron microscope by a hundred times. It is not impossible; it is not against the laws of diffraction of the electron. The wave length of the electron in such a microscope is only 1/20 of an angstrom. So it should be possible to see the individual atoms. What good would it be to see individual atoms distinctly?")]

#zh(id: "S025")[#text("如果我已经用每个比特5×5×5个原子的编码写好信息，问题是：今天怎样读出来？电子显微镜还不够好，即使极其小心、竭尽全力，也只能分辨大约10埃。我谈论这些微小尺度的事情时，想让各位深刻认识到，把电子显微镜提高100倍的重要性。这并非不可能，也不违背电子衍射的定律。在这样的显微镜里，电子的波长只有1/20埃，所以应该能够看到单个原子。清楚看见单个原子有什么用呢？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S026")[#text("We have friends in other fields — in biology, for instance. We physicists often look at them and say, “You know the reason you fellows are making so little progress?” (Actually I don’t know any field where they are making more rapid progress than they are in biology today.) “You should use more mathematics, like we do.” They could answer us — but they’re polite, so I’ll answer for them: “What you should do in order for us to make more rapid progress is to make the electron microscope 100 times better.”")]

#zh(id: "S026")[#text("我们在其他领域有朋友，比如生物学。物理学家常常看着他们说：‘你们知道为什么进展这么慢吗？’——事实上，我不知道今天还有哪个领域进步得比生物学更快。‘你们应该像我们一样，多用数学。’他们可以回答我们，但他们很有礼貌，所以我替他们回答：‘要让我们进步更快，你们应该做的，是把电子显微镜改进100倍。’")]

#html.p(id: "en-S027")[#text("What are the most central and fundamental problems of biology today? They are questions like: What is the sequence of bases in the DNA? What happens when you have a mutation? How is the base order in the DNA connected to the order of amino acids in the protein? What is the structure of the RNA; is it single-chain or double-chain, and how is it related in its order of bases to the DNA? What is the organization of the microsomes? How are proteins synthesized? Where does the RNA go? How does it sit? Where do the proteins sit? Where do the amino acids go in? In photosynthesis, where is the chlorophyll; how is it arranged; where are the carotenoids involved in this thing? What is the system of the conversion of light into chemical energy?")]

#zh(id: "S027")[#text("今天生物学最核心、最基础的问题是什么？比如：DNA中碱基的序列是什么？发生突变时出了什么事情？DNA中的碱基顺序怎样与蛋白质中的氨基酸顺序联系起来？RNA是什么结构，是单链还是双链，它的碱基顺序与DNA有什么关系？微粒体怎样组织？蛋白质怎样合成？RNA去哪里，怎样安置？蛋白质位于哪里？氨基酸从哪里进入？在光合作用中，叶绿素在哪里，怎样排列，类胡萝卜素参与其中的哪个环节？光转化成化学能的系统是怎样的？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S028")[#text("It is very easy to answer many of these fundamental biological questions; you just look at the thing! You will see the order of bases in the chain; you will see the structure of the microsome. Unfortunately, the present microscope sees at a scale which is just a bit too crude. Make the microscope one hundred times more powerful, and many problems of biology would be made very much easier. I exaggerate, of course, but the biologists would surely be very thankful to you — and they would prefer that to the criticism that they should use more mathematics.")]

#zh(id: "S028")[#text("许多这样的基础生物学问题，很容易回答：只要看一眼就行！你会看到链上的碱基顺序，看到微粒体的结构。遗憾的是，目前显微镜观察的尺度，恰好还粗糙了一点。把显微镜的能力提高100倍，许多生物学问题就会容易得多。当然，我是在夸张；但生物学家肯定会非常感谢你们，而且，比起批评他们应该多用数学，他们一定更喜欢这种帮助。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S029")[#text("The theory of chemical processes today is based on theoretical physics. In this sense, physics supplies the foundation of chemistry. But chemistry also has analysis. If you have a strange substance and you want to know what it is, you go through a long and complicated process of chemical analysis. You can analyze almost anything today, so I am a little late with my idea. But if the physicists wanted to, they could also dig under the chemists in the problem of chemical analysis. It would be very easy to make an analysis of any complicated chemical substance; all one would have to do would be to look at it and see where the atoms are. The only trouble is that the electron microscope is one hundred times too poor. (Later, I would like to ask the question: Can the physicists do something about the third problem of chemistry — namely, synthesis? Is there a physical way to synthesize any chemical substance?")]

#zh(id: "S029")[#text("今天的化学过程理论建立在理论物理学之上；从这个意义上说，物理学为化学提供基础。但化学还有分析。如果你有一种陌生物质，想知道它是什么，就得经历漫长而复杂的化学分析。今天几乎任何东西都能分析，所以我的想法来得有点晚。不过，物理学家如果愿意，也能在化学分析这个问题上深入到化学家下面。分析任何复杂化学物质都可以非常容易：只需看看它，看看原子在哪里。唯一的麻烦，是电子显微镜还差100倍。稍后我还想问：对于化学的第三个问题，即合成，物理学家能做些什么？有没有用物理方法合成任何化学物质的办法？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S030")[#text("The reason the electron microscope is so poor is that the f- value of the lenses is only 1 part to 1,000; you don’t have a big enough numerical aperture. And I know that there are theorems which prove that it is impossible, with axially symmetrical stationary field lenses, to produce an f-value any bigger than so and so; and therefore the resolving power at the present time is at its theoretical maximum. But in every theorem there are assumptions. Why must the field be symmetrical? I put this out as a challenge: Is there no way to make the electron microscope more powerful?")]

#zh(id: "S030")[#text("电子显微镜不够好的原因，是透镜的f值只有千分之一，数值孔径不够大。我知道，有些定理证明，用轴对称的静态场透镜，不可能把f值提高到某一限度以上，因此目前的分辨能力已经达到理论极限。但是，每个定理都有假设。为什么场一定要对称呢？我把这作为一项挑战提出来：难道没有办法让电子显微镜变得更强吗？")]

=== The marvelous biological system / 奇妙的生物系统

#html.p(id: "en-S032")[#text("The biological example of writing information on a small scale has inspired me to think of something that should be possible. Biology is not simply writing information; it is doing something about it. A biological system can be exceedingly small. Many of the cells are very tiny, but they are very active; they manufacture various substances; they walk around; they wiggle; and they do all kinds of marvelous things — all on a very small scale. Also, they store information. Consider the possibility that we too can make a thing very small which does what we want — that we can manufacture an object that maneuvers at that level!")]

#zh(id: "S032")[#text("生物在微小尺度上记录信息的例子，启发我想到一件应该可能实现的事。生物不仅记录信息，还根据它行动。一个生物系统可以极其微小。许多细胞很小，却非常活跃：制造各种物质，四处移动，扭动，做出种种奇妙的事情，而且都在非常小的尺度上进行。它们还存储信息。想一想，我们是否也能做出极小的东西，让它做我们想做的事，制造能在那个尺度上活动的物体！")]

#html.p(id: "en-S033")[#text("There may even be an economic point to this business of making things very small. Let me remind you of some of the problems of computing machines. In computers we have to store an enormous amount of information. The kind of writing that I was mentioning before, in which I had everything down as a distribution of metal, is permanent. Much more interesting to a computer is a way of writing, erasing, and writing something else. (This is usually because we don’t want to waste the material on which we have just written. Yet if we could write it in a very small space, it wouldn’t make any difference; it could just be thrown away after it was read. It doesn’t cost very much for the material).")]

#zh(id: "S033")[#text("把东西做得很小，也许还有经济意义。让我提醒各位计算机的一些问题。计算机必须存储海量信息。我前面说的，用金属分布来记录一切的写法，是永久性的。对计算机来说，更有意思的是能写入、擦去、再写别的内容的办法。这通常是因为，我们不想浪费刚刚写过的材料。然而，如果能在极小的空间里写入，这就没关系了：读完便扔掉也行，因为材料不值多少钱。")]

=== Miniaturizing the computer / 把计算机微型化

#html.p(id: "en-S035")[#text("I don’t know how to do this on a small scale in a practical way, but I do know that computing machines are very large; they fill rooms. Why can’t we make them very small, make them of little wires, little elements — and by little, I mean little. For instance, the wires should be 10 or 100 atoms in diameter, and the circuits should be a few thousand angstroms across. Everybody who has analyzed the logical theory of computers has come to the conclusion that the possibilities of computers are very interesting — if they could be made to be more complicated by several orders of magnitude. If they had millions of times as many elements, they could make judgments. They would have time to calculate what is the best way to make the calculation that they are about to make. They could select the method of analysis which, from their experience, is better than the one that we would give to them. And in many other ways, they would have new qualitative features.")]

#zh(id: "S035")[#text("我不知道怎样在微小尺度上切实做到这一点，但我知道，计算机非常庞大，装满整个房间。为什么不能把它们做得很小，用小导线、小元件来制造？我说小，就是真正的小。比如，导线直径应该只有10个或100个原子，电路的宽度应该只有几千埃。研究过计算机逻辑理论的人都得出结论：如果计算机能再复杂几个数量级，它的潜力会非常有意思。如果元件增加几百万倍，它们就能作判断；在开始某项计算之前，有时间先计算采用哪一种计算方法最好。它们能根据自己的经验，选出比我们指定的方法更好的分析方法。在许多其他方面，它们也会出现性质上的新特点。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S036")[#text("If I look at your face I immediately recognize that I have seen it before. (Actually, my friends will say I have chosen an unfortunate example here for the subject of this illustration. At least I recognize that it is a man and not an apple.) Yet there is no machine which, with that speed, can take a picture of a face and say even that it is a man; and much less that it is the same man that you showed it before — unless it is exactly the same picture. If the face is changed; if I am closer to the face; if I am further from the face; if the light changes — I recognize it anyway. Now, this little computer I carry in my head is easily able to do that. The computers that we build are not able to do that. The number of elements in this bone box of mine are enormously greater than the number of elements in our “wonderful” computers. But our mechanical computers are too big; the elements in this box are microscopic. I want to make some that are submicroscopic.")]

#zh(id: "S036")[#text("我看到你的脸，立刻认出以前见过。事实上，朋友们会说，为了说明这一点，我举了一个不太合适的例子；但至少我认得出，那是一个人，不是一个苹果。然而，没有哪台机器能以这样的速度，接收一张脸的图片，甚至判断它是个人；更不用说认出他就是以前给它看过的那个人，除非图片完全相同。即使脸变了，我靠近了或远离了，光线改变了，我仍能认出来。我脑袋里带着的这台小计算机，轻而易举就能做到；我们制造的计算机却做不到。我这个骨头盒子里的元件，比我们那些‘了不起的’计算机中的元件多得惊人。但我们的机械计算机太大；这个盒子里的元件是微观的。我想做一些比显微尺度还小的元件。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S037")[#text("If we wanted to make a computer that had all these marvelous extra qualitative abilities, we would have to make it, perhaps, the size of the Pentagon. This has several disadvantages. First, it requires too much material; there may not be enough germanium in the world for all the transistors which would have to be put into this enormous thing. There is also the problem of heat generation and power consumption; TVA would be needed to run the computer. But an even more practical difficulty is that the computer would be limited to a certain speed. Because of its large size, there is finite time required to get the information from one place to another. The information cannot go any faster than the speed of light — so, ultimately, when our computers get faster and faster and more and more elaborate, we will have to make them smaller and smaller.")]

#zh(id: "S037")[#text("如果我们想制造一台具有这些奇妙的额外能力、在性质上有所不同的计算机，也许得把它造得像五角大楼那么大。这有几个缺点。首先，材料太多；为了这台巨大机器中必须装入的全部晶体管，全世界的锗也许都不够用。还有发热和耗电问题：运行这台计算机，得动用田纳西河流域管理局的电力系统。但一个更实际的困难，是计算机的速度会受限。由于体积庞大，信息从一处传到另一处需要一定时间。信息不能超过光速，所以最终，随着计算机越来越快、越来越复杂，我们必须把它们做得越来越小。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S038")[#text("But there is plenty of room to make them smaller. There is nothing that I can see in the physical laws that says the computer elements cannot be made enormously smaller than they are now. In fact, there may be certain advantages.")]

#zh(id: "S038")[#text("而把它们做小，还有很大空间。我看不出，物理定律中的哪一条规定，计算机元件不能比现在小得多。事实上，这样做可能还有某些优点。")]

=== Miniaturization by evaporation / 通过蒸镀实现微型化

#html.p(id: "en-S040")[#text("How can we make such a device? What kind of manufacturing processes would we use? One possibility we might consider, since we have talked about writing by putting atoms down in a certain arrangement, would be to evaporate the material, then evaporate the insulator next to it. Then, for the next layer, evaporate another position of a wire, another insulator, and so on. So, you simply evaporate until you have a block of stuff which has the elements — coils and condensers, transistors and so on — of exceedingly fine dimensions.")]

#zh(id: "S040")[#text("怎样制造这样的装置？用什么制造工艺？既然已经谈到按特定排列放置原子来书写，一种可考虑的办法，就是蒸镀材料，再在旁边蒸镀绝缘体。到了下一层，再在另一个位置蒸镀导线，再加绝缘体，如此继续。这样不断蒸镀，最后便得到一块材料，其中含有尺寸极其细小的元件：线圈、电容、晶体管等等。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S041")[#text("But I would like to discuss, just for amusement, that there are other possibilities. Why can’t we manufacture these small computers somewhat like we manufacture the big ones? Why can’t we drill holes, cut things, solder things, stamp things out, mold different shapes all at an infinitesimal level? What are the limitations as to how small a thing has to be before you can no longer mold it? How many times when you are working on something frustratingly tiny like your wife’s wrist watch, have you said to yourself, “If I could only train an ant to do this!” What I would like to suggest is the possibility of training an ant to train a mite to do this. What are the possibilities of small but movable machines? They may or may not be useful, but they surely would be fun to make.")]

#zh(id: "S041")[#text("不过，纯粹为了好玩，我想谈谈其他可能。为什么不能像制造大计算机那样，制造小计算机？为什么不能在极微小的尺度上钻孔、切削、焊接、冲压、模制不同的形状？东西小到什么程度，才再也不能模制？修理妻子的手表之类小得令人恼火的东西时，你有多少次对自己说：‘要是能训练一只蚂蚁来干就好了！’我想提出的，是训练一只蚂蚁，再让它训练一只螨虫来干的可能性。微小而能活动的机器，能做些什么？它们也许有用，也许没用，但制造它们肯定很好玩。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S042")[#text("Consider any machine — for example, an automobile — and ask about the problems of making an infinitesimal machine like it. Suppose, in the particular design of the automobile, we need a certain precision of the parts; we need an accuracy, let’s suppose, of 4/10,000 of an inch. If things are more inaccurate than that in the shape of the cylinder and so on, it isn’t going to work very well. If I make the thing too small, I have to worry about the size of the atoms; I can’t make a circle of “balls” so to speak, if the circle is too small. So, if I make the error, corresponding to 4/10,000 of an inch, correspond to an error of 10 atoms, it turns out that I can reduce the dimensions of an automobile 4,000 times, approximately — so that it is 1 mm. across. Obviously, if you redesign the car so that it would work with a much larger tolerance, which is not at all impossible, then you could make a much smaller device.")]

#zh(id: "S042")[#text("想想任何一种机器，例如汽车，再问：制造一台像它那样的微小机器，会遇到什么问题？假设这种汽车的具体设计要求零件具有一定精度，比如精确到4/10000英寸。如果气缸等部件的形状误差超过这个限度，它就不能很好地工作。如果把东西做得太小，我就必须担心原子的大小；可以这样说，圆如果太小，就无法用‘小球’围成它。假如把相当于4/10000英寸的误差，缩减为10个原子的误差，计算结果表明，汽车的尺寸大约能缩小4000倍，变成1毫米宽。显然，如果重新设计汽车，让它能在更大公差下工作——这完全不是不可能——就能做出小得多的装置。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S043")[#text("It is interesting to consider what the problems are in such small machines. Firstly, with parts stressed to the same degree, the forces go as the area you are reducing, so that things like weight and inertia are of relatively no importance. The strength of material, in other words, is very much greater in proportion. The stresses and expansion of the flywheel from centrifugal force, for example, would be the same proportion only if the rotational speed is increased in the same proportion as we decrease the size. On the other hand, the metals that we use have a grain structure, and this would be very annoying at small scale because the material is not homogeneous. Plastics and glass and things of this amorphous nature are very much more homogeneous, and so we would have to make our machines out of such materials.")]

#zh(id: "S043")[#text("考虑这种小机器的问题，很有意思。首先，若零件承受相同的应力，力会随着缩小后的面积而变化，因此重量和惯性之类，相对来说就不那么重要。换句话说，材料的强度相对于它们会大得多。比如，只有在尺寸缩小多少倍、转速就提高多少倍时，飞轮因离心力产生的应力和膨胀，才会保持同样的比例。另一方面，我们使用的金属具有晶粒结构；在微小尺度上，这会非常麻烦，因为材料不均匀。塑料、玻璃以及这类非晶材料均匀得多，所以必须用这样的材料制造机器。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S044")[#text("There are problems associated with the electrical part of the system — with the copper wires and the magnetic parts. The magnetic properties on a very small scale are not the same as on a large scale; there is the “domain” problem involved. A big magnet made of millions of domains can only be made on a small scale with one domain. The electrical equipment won’t simply be scaled down; it has to be redesigned. But I can see no reason why it can’t be redesigned to work again.")]

#zh(id: "S044")[#text("系统的电气部分也有问题，比如铜线和磁性部件。微小尺度上的磁性，与大尺度上不同，这里涉及‘磁畴’问题。由几百万个磁畴组成的大磁铁，缩小后只能成为一个磁畴。电气设备不能直接按比例缩小，必须重新设计。但我看不出有什么理由，重新设计后它就不能工作。")]

=== Problems of lubrication / 润滑问题

#html.p(id: "en-S046")[#text("Lubrication involves some interesting points. The effective viscosity of oil would be higher and higher in proportion as we went down (and if we increase the speed as much as we can). If we don’t increase the speed so much, and change from oil to kerosene or some other fluid, the problem is not so bad. But actually we may not have to lubricate at all! We have a lot of extra force. Let the bearings run dry; they won’t run hot because the heat escapes away from such a small device very, very rapidly.")]

#zh(id: "S046")[#text("润滑有一些有趣的地方。随着尺寸缩小，如果我们把速度尽可能提高，油的有效黏度会相对越来越高。若不把速度提高那么多，并且把油换成煤油或其他液体，问题就不那么严重。但实际上，我们可能根本不需要润滑！我们有许多额外的力。让轴承干转好了；它们不会发热，因为热从这样小的装置上散失得非常、非常快。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S047")[#text("This rapid heat loss would prevent the gasoline from exploding, so an internal combustion engine is impossible. Other chemical reactions, liberating energy when cold, can be used. Probably an external supply of electrical power would be most convenient for such small machines.")]

#zh(id: "S047")[#text("这种迅速散热会阻止汽油爆发燃烧，所以不能使用内燃机。可以采用在低温下释放能量的其他化学反应。对于这种小机器，外部供给电力大概最方便。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S048")[#text("What would be the utility of such machines? Who knows? Of course, a small automobile would only be useful for the mites to drive around in, and I suppose our Christian interests don’t go that far. However, we did note the possibility of the manufacture of small elements for computers in completely automatic factories, containing lathes and other machine tools at the very small level. The small lathe would not have to be exactly like our big lathe. I leave to your imagination the improvement of the design to take full advantage of the properties of things on a small scale, and in such a way that the fully automatic aspect would be easiest to manage.")]

#zh(id: "S048")[#text("这样的机器有什么用？谁知道呢？当然，小汽车只适合给螨虫开着四处跑，我想，作为基督徒，我们的关怀还没到那个地步。不过，我们已经提到，在完全自动化、包含微型车床和其他机床的工厂里，制造计算机小元件的可能性。小车床不必与大车床完全相同。我把设计上的改进留给你们想象：充分利用微小尺度上事物的性质，同时使全面自动化最容易实现。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S049")[#text("A friend of mine (Albert R. Hibbs) suggests a very interesting possibility for relatively small machines. He says that, although it is a very wild idea, it would be interesting in surgery if you could swallow the surgeon. You put the mechanical surgeon inside the blood vessel and it goes into the heart and “looks” around. (Of course the information has to be fed out.) It finds out which valve is the faulty one and takes a little knife and slices it out. Other small machines might be permanently incorporated in the body to assist some inadequately-functioning organ.")]

#zh(id: "S049")[#text("我的一位朋友，阿尔伯特·希布斯，为相对微小的机器提出了一个很有意思的可能。他说，虽然这想法非常异想天开，但如果能把外科医生吞下去，外科手术就会很有意思。把机械外科医生放进血管，它进入心脏，四处‘查看’；当然，信息必须传出来。它找出哪个瓣膜有问题，拿出一把小刀，将它切除。其他小机器还可以永久装在体内，帮助某个功能不足的器官。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S050")[#text("Now comes the interesting question: How do we make such a tiny mechanism? I leave that to you. However, let me suggest one weird possibility. You know, in the atomic energy plants they have materials and machines that they can’t handle directly because they have become radioactive. To unscrew nuts and put on bolts and so on, they have a set of master and slave hands, so that by operating a set of levers here, you control the “hands” there, and can turn them this way and that so you can handle things quite nicely.")]

#zh(id: "S050")[#text("现在出现了一个有趣的问题：怎样制造这样微小的机构？我把这个问题留给你们。不过，让我提出一种古怪的可能。你们知道，原子能工厂里有些材料和机器具有放射性，不能直接接触。为了拧下螺母、装上螺栓等等，人们配备了主手和从手。操作这里的一组操纵杆，就可以控制那里的‘手’，向各个方向转动，相当方便地处理东西。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S051")[#text("Most of these devices are actually made rather simply, in that there is a particular cable, like a marionette string, that goes directly from the controls to the “hands.” But, of course, things also have been made using servo motors, so that the connection between the one thing and the other is electrical rather than mechanical. When you turn the levers, they turn a servo motor, and it changes the electrical currents in the wires, which repositions a motor at the other end.")]

#zh(id: "S051")[#text("这些装置大多造得相当简单：有一根像牵线木偶的线那样的特定钢索，从控制器直接连到‘手’。当然，也已经有采用伺服电动机的装置，使两端通过电而不是机械连接。转动操纵杆时，它带动一个伺服电动机，改变导线中的电流，从而改变另一端电动机的位置。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S052")[#text("Now, I want to build much the same device — a master-slave system which operates electrically. But I want the slaves to be made especially carefully by modern large-scale machinists so that they are one-fourth the scale of the “hands” that you ordinarily maneuver. So you have a scheme by which you can do things at one- quarter scale anyway — the little servo motors with little hands play with little nuts and bolts; they drill little holes; they are four times smaller. Aha! So I manufacture a quarter-size lathe; I manufacture quarter-size tools; and I make, at the one-quarter scale, still another set of hands again relatively one-quarter size! This is one-sixteenth size, from my point of view. And after I finish doing this I wire directly from my large-scale system, through transformers perhaps, to the one-sixteenth-size servo motors. Thus I can now manipulate the one-sixteenth size hands.")]

#zh(id: "S052")[#text("现在，我想造一个差不多的装置：电动的主从系统。但我要让从手由现代大型机械加工人员特别精心地制造，尺寸只有通常操纵的‘手’的四分之一。这样，至少就有了能在四分之一尺度上做事的方案：小伺服电动机带着小手，操作小螺母和小螺栓，钻小孔，所有东西都缩小四倍。啊哈！于是我制造一台四分之一尺寸的车床，制造四分之一尺寸的工具，再在这个尺度上，制造另一套相对又缩小到四分之一的手。从我的角度看，这就是十六分之一尺寸。完成以后，把大尺度系统的导线直接接到十六分之一尺寸的伺服电动机，也许中间通过变压器。于是，我就可以操纵十六分之一尺寸的手。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S053")[#text("Well, you get the principle from there on. It is rather a difficult program, but it is a possibility. You might say that one can go much farther in one step than from one to four. Of course, this has all to be designed very carefully and it is not necessary simply to make it like hands. If you thought of it very carefully, you could probably arrive at a much better system for doing such things.")]

#zh(id: "S053")[#text("好，接下来的原理，各位已经明白。这是一项相当困难的计划，但确有可能。你们也许会说，一步能缩小的幅度，远不止从一到四。当然，一切都必须十分仔细地设计，也不必简单地照着人手来做。认真考虑之后，很可能找到做这些事的更好的系统。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S054")[#text("If you work through a pantograph, even today, you can get much more than a factor of four in even one step. But you can’t work directly through a pantograph which makes a smaller pantograph which then makes a smaller pantograph — because of the looseness of the holes and the irregularities of construction. The end of the pantograph wiggles with a relatively greater irregularity than the irregularity with which you move your hands. In going down this scale, I would find the end of the pantograph on the end of the pantograph on the end of the pantograph shaking so badly that it wasn’t doing anything sensible at all.")]

#zh(id: "S054")[#text("即使在今天，利用缩放仪，一步也能缩小远超过四倍。但你不能直接通过一台缩放仪制造更小的缩放仪，再由它制造更小的一台；因为孔洞存在松动，构造也有不规则之处。缩放仪末端的晃动，相对而言，会比你的手的运动误差更大。沿着这个尺度不断缩小，在缩放仪末端再接缩放仪，再接一台，最终末端会抖得如此厉害，根本做不了任何像样的事情。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S055")[#text("At each stage, it is necessary to improve the precision of the apparatus. If, for instance, having made a small lathe with a pantograph, we find its lead screw irregular — more irregular than the large-scale one — we could lap the lead screw against breakable nuts that you can reverse in the usual way back and forth until this lead screw is, at its scale, as accurate as our original lead screws, at our scale.")]

#zh(id: "S055")[#text("每一步都必须提高装置的精度。例如，用缩放仪做出一台小车床后，若发现它的丝杠不规则，而且比大车床的丝杠更不规则，我们可以用可拆分螺母对丝杠进行研磨，按通常的方法反复来回换向，直到这根丝杠在自己的尺度上，与原始丝杠在我们的尺度上一样精确。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S056")[#text("We can make flats by rubbing unflat surfaces in triplicates together — in three pairs — and the flats then become flatter than the thing you started with. Thus, it is not impossible to improve precision on a small scale by the correct operations. So, when we build this stuff, it is necessary at each step to improve the accuracy of the equipment by working for awhile down there, making accurate lead screws, Johansen blocks, and all the other materials which we use in accurate machine work at the higher level. We have to stop at each level and manufacture all the stuff to go to the next level — a very long and very difficult program. Perhaps you can figure a better way than that to get down to small scale more rapidly.")]

#zh(id: "S056")[#text("把三个不平的表面两两配对、相互研磨，可以制成平面，所得平面会比起初更平。因此，靠正确的操作，提高微小尺度上的精度并非不可能。制造这些东西时，每一步都需要在那个尺度上工作一段时间，做出精密丝杠、约翰逊量块，以及大尺度精密机械加工所使用的其他一切材料，借此提高设备的精度。每到一个尺度，都必须停下来，制造通向下一尺度所需的全部东西；这是一项非常漫长、非常困难的计划。也许你们能想出比这更好的办法，更快地进入微小尺度。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S057")[#text("Yet, after all this, you have just got one little baby lathe four thousand times smaller than usual. But we were thinking of making an enormous computer, which we were going to build by drilling holes on this lathe to make little washers for the computer. How many washers can you manufacture on this one lathe?")]

#zh(id: "S057")[#text("然而，经历这一切之后，你只得到一台比通常车床小4000倍的小小车床。我们原来想制造的是一台庞大的计算机，打算用这台车床钻孔，制造计算机的小垫圈。一台这样的车床，能造出多少垫圈呢？")]

=== A hundred tiny hands / 一百双微小的手

#html.p(id: "en-S059")[#text("When I make my first set of slave “hands” at one-fourth scale, I am going to make ten sets. I make ten sets of “hands,” and I wire them to my original levers so they each do exactly the same thing at the same time in parallel. Now, when I am making my new devices one-quarter again as small, I let each one manufacture ten copies, so that I would have a hundred “hands” at the 1/16th size.")]

#zh(id: "S059")[#text("制造第一套四分之一尺度的从手时，我要造十套。我造出十套‘手’，把它们接到原来的操纵杆上，让每套在同一时间，完全平行地做同一件事。然后，制造再缩小到四分之一的新装置时，让每套都制造十份，于是就得到一百套十六分之一尺寸的‘手’。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S060")[#text("Where am I going to put the million lathes that I am going to have? Why, there is nothing to it; the volume is much less than that of even one full-scale lathe. For instance, if I made a billion little lathes, each 1/4000 of the scale of a regular lathe, there are plenty of materials and space available because in the billion little ones there is less than 2 percent of the materials in one big lathe.")]

#zh(id: "S060")[#text("将来那一百万台车床，放在哪里呢？这根本不是问题：它们的总体积，甚至远小于一台全尺寸车床。比如，如果制造十亿台小车床，每台都是普通车床的1/4000尺度，材料和空间都绰绰有余，因为十亿台小车床的总用料，还不到一台大车床的2%。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S061")[#text("It doesn’t cost anything for materials, you see. So I want to build a billion tiny factories, models of each other, which are manufacturing simultaneously, drilling holes, stamping parts, and so on.")]

#zh(id: "S061")[#text("你们看，材料几乎不花钱。因此，我想造十亿座微型工厂，彼此互为模型，同时制造、钻孔、冲压零件等等。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S062")[#text("As we go down in size, there are a number of interesting problems that arise. All things do not simply scale down in proportion. There is the problem that materials stick together by the molecular (Van der Waals) attractions. It would be like this: After you have made a part and you unscrew the nut from a bolt, it isn’t going to fall down because the gravity isn’t appreciable; it would even be hard to get it off the bolt. It would be like those old movies of a man with his hands full of molasses, trying to get rid of a glass of water. There will be several problems of this nature that we will have to be ready to design for.")]

#zh(id: "S062")[#text("随着尺寸缩小，会出现一些有趣的问题。并非一切都能简单地按比例缩小。材料会因分子间的范德华吸引力黏在一起。情形可能如下：做出一个零件后，把螺母从螺栓上拧下来，它却不会掉落，因为重力已经微不足道；甚至很难把它从螺栓上取下。这就像老电影里，一个人满手糖浆，想摆脱一只水杯。我们必须准备在设计中应对几个这样的难题。")]

=== Rearranging the atoms / 重新排列原子

#html.p(id: "en-S064")[#text("But I am not afraid to consider the final question as to whether, ultimately — in the great future — we can arrange the atoms the way we want; the very atoms, all the way down! What would happen if we could arrange the atoms one by one the way we want them (within reason, of course; you can’t put them so that they are chemically unstable, for example).")]

#zh(id: "S064")[#text("但我不怕考虑最终的问题：在遥远的未来，我们终究能不能按照意愿排列原子？一直深入到底，操纵原子本身！如果能逐个把原子排成我们想要的样子，会怎样？当然要在合理范围内，比如不能把它们放成化学上不稳定的结构。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S065")[#text("Up to now, we have been content to dig in the ground to find minerals. We heat them and we do things on a large scale with them, and we hope to get a pure substance with just so much impurity, and so on. But we must always accept some atomic arrangement that nature gives us. We haven’t got anything, say, with a “checkerboard” arrangement, with the impurity atoms exactly arranged 1,000 angstroms apart, or in some other particular pattern.")]

#zh(id: "S065")[#text("到目前为止，我们满足于从地下挖出矿物，加热它们，在大尺度上加工，希望得到一种只含特定数量杂质的纯物质，等等。但我们总得接受大自然给定的某种原子排列。我们还没有哪种材料，比如具有‘棋盘格’排列，使杂质原子恰好相隔1000埃，或者呈现其他特定图案。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S066")[#text("What could we do with layered structures with just the right layers? What would the properties of materials be if we could really arrange the atoms the way we want them? They would be very interesting to investigate theoretically. I can’t see exactly what would happen, but I can hardly doubt that when we have some control of the arrangement of things on a small scale we will get an enormously greater range of possible properties that substances can have, and of different things that we can do.")]

#zh(id: "S066")[#text("如果能获得层次恰到好处的层状结构，我们能做些什么？如果真正能按意愿排列原子，材料会有什么性质？理论上研究这些问题，会很有意思。我不能准确预见会发生什么；但几乎毫不怀疑，一旦在微小尺度上对排列获得某种控制，物质可能具有的性质，以及我们能够做的不同事情，都会有大得多的范围。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S067")[#text("Consider, for example, a piece of material in which we make little coils and condensers (or their solid state analogs) 1,000 or 10,000 angstroms in a circuit, one right next to the other, over a large area, with little antennas sticking out at the other end — a whole series of circuits. Is it possible, for example, to emit light from a whole set of antennas, like we emit radio waves from an organized set of antennas to beam the radio programs to Europe? The same thing would be to beam the light out in a definite direction with very high intensity. (Perhaps such a beam is not very useful technically or economically.)")]

#zh(id: "S067")[#text("例如，考虑一块材料：在大面积上，把宽1000埃或10000埃、含有小线圈和小电容，或其固态对应物的电路，紧挨着排列；另一端伸出小天线，组成整片电路。比如，能否让整套天线发光，就像用有组织的天线阵列发出无线电波，把广播节目传到欧洲一样？同样的办法，可以让光沿特定方向，以极高的强度发射出去。这样的光束，也许在技术或经济上没有很大用处。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S068")[#text("I have thought about some of the problems of building electric circuits on a small scale, and the problem of resistance is serious. If you build a corresponding circuit on a small scale, its natural frequency goes up, since the wave length goes down as the scale; but the skin depth only decreases with the square root of the scale ratio, and so resistive problems are of increasing difficulty. Possibly we can beat resistance through the use of superconductivity if the frequency is not too high, or by other tricks.")]

#zh(id: "S068")[#text("我考虑过在微小尺度上制造电路的一些问题，电阻是个严重问题。把相应电路缩小后，其固有频率会上升，因为波长随尺寸同比缩小；但是趋肤深度只随尺度比的平方根减小，因此电阻问题会越来越困难。频率如果不太高，也许可以通过超导克服电阻，或者采用其他技巧。")]

=== Atoms in a small world / 微小世界中的原子

#html.p(id: "en-S070")[#text("When we get to the very, very small world — say circuits of seven atoms — we have a lot of new things that would happen that represent completely new opportunities for design. Atoms on a small scale behave like nothing on a large scale, for they satisfy the laws of quantum mechanics. So, as we go down and fiddle around with the atoms down there, we are working with different laws, and we can expect to do different things. We can manufacture in different ways. We can use, not just circuits, but some system involving the quantized energy levels, or the interactions of quantized spins, etc.")]

#zh(id: "S070")[#text("进入非常、非常微小的世界，比如只有七个原子大小的电路时，会发生许多新事情，带来完全新的设计机会。微小尺度上的原子，与大尺度上的任何事物都不同，因为它们服从量子力学。因此，当我们深入下去，在那里摆弄原子时，就在按照不同的规律工作，可以预期做到不同的事情。制造方法也可以不同。我们不仅可以使用电路，还可以使用涉及量子化能级、量子化自旋相互作用等的某种系统。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S071")[#text("Another thing we will notice is that, if we go down far enough, all of our devices can be mass produced so that they are absolutely perfect copies of one another. We cannot build two large machines so that the dimensions are exactly the same. But if your machine is only 100 atoms high, you only have to get it correct to one-half of one percent to make sure the other machine is exactly the same size — namely, 100 atoms high!")]

#zh(id: "S071")[#text("另一件会注意到的事是，只要尺度缩小得足够多，全部装置都可以批量生产，而且成为彼此绝对完美的复制品。我们不能把两台大机器制造得尺寸完全一样；但如果机器只有100个原子高，只需把误差控制在百分之半，就能确保另一台机器尺寸完全相同：同样高100个原子！")]

#html.p(id: "en-S072")[#text("At the atomic level, we have new kinds of forces and new kinds of possibilities, new kinds of effects. The problems of manufacture and reproduction of materials will be quite different. I am, as I said, inspired by the biological phenomena in which chemical forces are used in repetitious fashion to produce all kinds of weird effects (one of which is the author).")]

#zh(id: "S072")[#text("在原子尺度上，有新种类的力、新的可能性和新的效应。材料的制造与复制问题，将与以往很不一样。正如我说过，我受到生物现象的启发：它们反复利用化学作用力，产生种种古怪的结果，作者本人就是其中之一。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S073")[#text("The principles of physics, as far as I can see, do not speak against the possibility of maneuvering things atom by atom. It is not an attempt to violate any laws; it is something, in principle, that can be done; but in practice, it has not been done because we are too big.")]

#zh(id: "S073")[#text("据我所见，物理学原理并不反对逐个原子地操纵事物。这不是要违反什么定律，而是原则上能够做到的事；只是实践中还没有做到，因为我们太大了。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S074")[#text("Ultimately, we can do chemical synthesis. A chemist comes to us and says, “Look, I want a molecule that has the atoms arranged thus and so; make me that molecule.” The chemist does a mysterious thing when he wants to make a molecule. He sees that it has got that ring, so he mixes this and that, and he shakes it, and he fiddles around. And, at the end of a difficult process, he usually does succeed in synthesizing what he wants. By the time I get my devices working, so that we can do it by physics, he will have figured out how to synthesize absolutely anything, so that this will really be useless.")]

#zh(id: "S074")[#text("最终，我们可以做化学合成。一位化学家来对我们说：‘看，我要一种分子，原子这样这样排列；替我做出这个分子。’化学家想制造分子时，做的事情很神秘。他看出分子有那个环，于是混合这个和那个，摇一摇，摆弄一番。经过艰难的过程，最后通常真能合成想要的东西。等到我把装置运转起来，让我们可以用物理方法做到这些时，他早已找到了合成任何东西的办法，所以这件事其实就没有用了。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S075")[#text("But it is interesting that it would be, in principle, possible (I think) for a physicist to synthesize any chemical substance that the chemist writes down. Give the orders and the physicist synthesizes it. How? Put the atoms down where the chemist says, and so you make the substance. The problems of chemistry and biology can be greatly helped if our ability to see what we are doing, and to do things on an atomic level, is ultimately developed — a development which I think cannot be avoided.")]

#zh(id: "S075")[#text("但有意思的是，原则上，我认为，物理学家能够合成化学家写出的任何化学物质。给出指令，物理学家就把它合成出来。怎么做？把原子放到化学家指定的位置，物质就做出来了。如果我们最终发展出看清自己在做什么、并在原子尺度上动手操作的能力，就能给化学和生物学问题提供极大的帮助。我认为，这样的发展不可避免。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S076")[#text("Now, you might say, “Who should do this and why should they do it?” Well, I pointed out a few of the economic applications, but I know that the reason that you would do it might be just for fun. But have some fun! Let’s have a competition between laboratories. Let one laboratory make a tiny motor which it sends to another lab which sends it back with a thing that fits inside the shaft of the first motor.")]

#zh(id: "S076")[#text("现在，你们也许会问：‘谁应该来做，为什么要做？’我已经提到几项经济应用，但我知道，你们也许仅仅为了好玩，就愿意去做。那就玩一玩吧！让实验室之间展开竞赛。一个实验室造一台微型电动机，寄给另一个实验室；另一个再寄回来一件东西，能装进第一台电动机的轴里。")]

=== High school competition / 高中生竞赛

#html.p(id: "en-S078")[#text("Just for the fun of it, and in order to get kids interested in this field, I would propose that someone who has some contact with the high schools think of making some kind of high school competition. After all, we haven’t even started in this field, and even the kids can write smaller than has ever been written before. They could have competition in high schools. The Los Angeles high school could send a pin to the Venice high school on which it says, “How’s this?” They get the pin back, and in the dot of the “i” it says, “Not so hot.”")]

#zh(id: "S078")[#text("纯粹为了好玩，也为了让孩子们对这个领域感兴趣，我建议，有人若与高中有联系，就考虑办某种高中生竞赛。毕竟，我们在这个领域还没有起步，连孩子都能写出比以往任何文字更小的字。他们可以在高中之间比赛。洛杉矶高中寄给威尼斯高中一枚针，针上写着‘这个怎么样？’等针寄回来，字母‘i’的那个点里又写着‘不怎么样。’")]

#html.p(id: "en-S079")[#text("Perhaps this doesn’t excite you to do it, and only economics will do so. Then I want to do something; but I can’t do it at the present moment, because I haven’t prepared the ground. It is my intention to offer a prize of $1,000 to the first guy who can take the information on the page of a book and put it on an area 1/25,000 smaller in linear scale in such manner that it can be read by an electron microscope.")]

#zh(id: "S079")[#text("也许，这还不足以让你们兴奋起来动手，只有经济利益才可以。那么，我想做一件事，但眼下还做不了，因为准备尚未做好。我打算设立1000美元奖金，奖给第一个把一页书的信息，按线性尺寸缩小到1/25000的比例，写到相应面积上，而且能用电子显微镜读出来的人。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S080")[#text("And I want to offer another prize — if I can figure out how to phrase it so that I don’t get into a mess of arguments about definitions — of another $1,000 to the first guy who makes an operating electric motor — a rotating electric motor which can be controlled from the outside and, not counting the lead-in wires, is only 1/64 inch cube.")]

#zh(id: "S080")[#text("我还想再设一项1000美元奖金，奖给第一个造出可运转电动机的人：一台旋转式电动机，可以从外部控制；不计引入导线，整个机体只有边长1/64英寸的立方体那么大。不过，我得先想好如何表述条件，免得陷入关于定义的无穷争论。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S081")[#text("I do not expect that such prizes will have to wait very long for claimants.")]

#zh(id: "S081")[#text("我想，这两项奖金不会等很久才有人来领取。")]
